# Compartmentalization within Self-Assembled Metal-Organic Framework Nanoparticles for Tandem Reactions

## abstract
Compartmentalization is an essential feature found in living cells to ensure multiple biological processes occur without being affected by undesired external influences. Here, compartmentalized systems are developed based on the self-assembly of metal-organic framework (MOF) nanoparticles into multifunctional MOF capsules (MOF-Cs). Such MOF-Cs have the capability of controlling molecular transportation and protecting interior microenvironment, thus making tandem reaction along trajectories to desired products. First of all, MOF-Cs present controlled molecular transportation derived from molecular sieving property of MOFs. Second, MOF-Cs can protect the encapsulated cargoes from denaturation and maintain their catalytic activity. Third, MOF-Cs can provide spatial segregation for incompatible species and facilitate communication between these compartments to perform tandem reactions. These compartmentalized structures offer new views in the transportation, microreactor, and biotechnology.

Compartmentalization is an essential feature found in living cells to ensure multiple biological processes occur without being affected by undesired external influences. Here, compartmentalized systems are developed based on the self-assembly of metal–organic framework (MOF) nanoparticles into multifunctional MOF capsules (MOF-Cs). Such MOF-Cs have the capability of controlling molecular transportation and protecting interior microenvironment, thus making tandem reaction along trajectories to desired products. First of all, MOF-Cs present controlled molecular transportation derived from molecular sieving property of MOFs. Second, MOF-Cs can protect the encapsulated cargoes from denaturation and maintain their catalytic activity. Third, MOF-Cs can provide spatial segregation for incompatible species and facilitate communication between these compartments to perform tandem reactions. These compartmentalized structures offer new views in the transportation, microreactor, and biotechnology.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a kind of interesting porous materials, which are built from metal ions/clusters coordinated with organic ligands.[1] Owing to their large surface areas, uniform but tunable cavities, and tailorable chemistry, MOFs have been widely used for a variety of applications, such as gas storage,[2] separation,[3] catalysis,[4] chemical sensing,[5] and drug delivery.[6] In fact, the flexible choice of inorganic– organic building blocks endows MOFs with a tunable microenvironment.[7] In addition, due to the uniform pore size, MOFs exhibit unique performance in selective catalysis and separation.[8] Thus, MOFs are the promising candidates for the construction of multifunctional compartmentalized structures to regulate molecular transportation and protect interior microenvironment.[9]
Compartmentalization is one of the key features found in living cells that enables spatiotemporal control over multistep biological processes.[10] Compartmentalization of an interior environment prevents undesired interference and enables regulation of molecular transportation.[11] Several self-assembled structures have been used as compartmentalized systems such as liposomes,[12] polymersomes,[13] and colloidosomes.[14] Both liposomes and polymersomes provide an aqueous enclosed for encapsulated reactions yet exhibit substantial drawbacks, i.e., suboptimal mechanical stability of liposomes,   
stringent design criteria of polymersomes, hindering diffu  
sion of solutes to and from the bulk solution.[15] Based on the   
self-assembly process of inorganic nanoparticles (NPs), colloi  
dosomes should exhibit greater chemical stability, mechanical   
robustness, and membrane functionality derived from the NPs   
as building blocks. Because the permeability of colloidosomes   
has been tailored by the interstices between the NPs, it is difficult   
to regulate the controlled diffusion of small molecules, limiting   
the selective molecular transport.[16] Compared with the above   
capsules, the uniform but tunable pore size of MOFs endows   
MOF capsules with much potential in controlled transport and   
compartmentalization effect.[17] Recently, a few efforts have been   
devoted to preparing MOF capsules.[18] One is the MOF growth   
and spontaneous assembly into MOF capsules. Zeng’s group   
has developed for the first time a one-step emulsion-based   
technique for the fabrication of submicrometer-sized mono  
disperse Fe-soc-MOF cubes and their sub­sequent integration   
into 3D superstructures.[19] It would be more interesting if this   
strategy can extend to more kinds of MOFs with stable struc  
ture. However, the rigid synthesis conditions of some MOFs   
such as high temperatures $(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ or acidic/alkaline condi  
tions may affect the activity of the encapsulated biomolecules.   
Another is the self-assembly of presynthesized MOF particles   
into MOF capsules. Bradshaw’ group has first reported MOF  
polymer composite microcapsules by immobilizing preformed   
MOF NPs assembled at the Pickering emulsion into a poly­   
styrene membrane.[20] Then they demonstrated the hierarchically   
porous MOF-based capsules for size-selective biocatalysis. Such
selective catalysis was achieved by employing a time-consuming approach, which was the assembly of UiO-66 NPs around emulsion droplets with the further deposition of ZIF-8.[21] Therefore, developing a facile strategy to construct MOF capsules in order to simultaneously realize efficient encapsulation, controlled transport, compartmentalization effect, and communication capabilities remains a considerable challenge.
Here, we develop a facile strategy to structure MOF capsules (MOF-Cs) by the self-assembly process of MOF NPs in the oilwater surface and using polymer precipitation as a support layer for enhancing stability. The obtained MOF-Cs own the MOFs layer for controlling molecular transportation, the hollow structure for supplying confined reaction space, and the ability of encapsulating diverse species for offering catalytic active sites. First and foremost, MOF-Cs exhibit controlled transport originated from size-sieving ability of MOFs. Second, MOF-Cs can keep the encapsulated cargoes from denaturation and preserve their catalytic activity. Third, MOF-Cs can provide spatial segregation for incompatible species. Furthermore, MOF-Cs separately encapsulated with incompatible species are capable of communicating with each other to perform multistep reactions.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis of MOF-Cs
MOF-Cs are acquired through the self-assembly of MOF NPs in the oil–water surface and polymer deposition inside capsules (Scheme 1). Initially, water-in-oil Pickering emulsions that consisted of aqueous droplets stabilized by hydrophobic MOF NPs were prepared in dodecane through the shear force of homogenizer (step i). Then, the emulsions were evaporated to generate polymer deposition inside the capsules (step ii). In this case, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ NPs were taken as an example to study the whole preparation procedure of MOF-Cs. UiO- $66\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdot}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ NPs were synthesized through the reaction mixture of zirconium chloride, amino-terephthalic acid, and benzoic acid dissolved in dimethyl­ formamide (DMF) at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .[22] Characterized with spherical morphology, the synthesized UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ NPs had an average diameter of $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1B). Moreover, the postsynthetic method was used to tune the hydrophobicity of MOFs through the reaction between the heptanoic anhydride and the dangling amino groups.[23] From the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curves of modified MOF NPs, the degree of surface modification upon reaction with heptanoic anhydride was about $20.0\%$ (Figure S1 and Table S1, Supporting Information). The modified MOF NPs show no obvious changes in morphology and size (Figure 1C). The $120^{\circ}$ contact angle indicated the hydrophobicity of the modified MOF NPs (Figure 1D), which make the modified MOF NPs have sufficient tendency to stabilize the water-in-oil emulsions. As illustrated in Figure 1A, water-in-oil Pickering emulsions stabilized by hydrophobic MOF NPs were first prepared in dodecane via the shear force of homogenizer (step i). To enhance the stability of MOF-Cs, the polymer (polymethyl methacrylate, PMMA) was deposited inside MOF-Cs by solvent evaporation (step ii).[24] There were a large number of MOF-Cs in the low resolution overview image (Figure  1E).
The MOF-Cs were about $10{-}15~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ in diameter. Among these, a representative MOF-C showed highly spherical shapes similar to their building block NPs (Figure  1F). The surface image of the representative MOF-Cs clearly demonstrates that the modified MOF NPs are successfully assembled to form MOF-Cs (Figure 1G). The ratio of PMMA in MOF-Cs was about $44.0\%$ from the TGA curve of MOF-Cs (Figure  S2A and Table S2, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure S2B in the Supporting Information, the MOF-Cs apparently comprised binary layers: PMMA layer and MOF layer. The PMMA layer was about $70\ \mathrm{nm}$ . PMMA was deposited inside MOF-Cs, filling among the MOF NPs. The MOF NPs were embedded within the exterior surface thus facilitating molecular transport. To further investigate the structure of MOF-Cs, PMMA capsules (PMMA-Cs) were chosen as a comparison. PMMA-Cs with spherical shapes were obtained by following the similar procedure as described in Figure 1A with MOF NPs being replaced by Span 80 (Figure  S3, Supporting Information). In addition, MOF-Cs presented similar X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns with the simulated $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Figures S4 and S5, Supporting Information), suggesting that MOF-Cs maintain the crystalline structure of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ .
## 2.2. Hierarchical Pore Structure of MOF-Cs
MOF-Cs exhibited hierarchical pore structures through the assembly of MOF NPs. The synthesized and hydrophobic UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ NPs both displayed type I isotherms, with steep increase in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake at a low relative pressure, revealing their characteristic microporosity (Figure  S5, Supporting Information). Compared with the synthesized UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ NPs $(646~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , the modified UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ NPs $(348~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ showed a decrease in gravimetric Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas owing to the postsynthetic modification by heptanoic anhydride. The obtained MOF-Cs demonstrated hierarchical pore structures (Figure  S6, Supporting Information). The microporosity was induced by $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ NPs at the surface of MOF-Cs under low relative pressure. And the appearance of a hysteresis loop at the medium relative pressure exhibited the existence of mesoporosity, which may be attributed to the interstice between the self-assembled MOF NPs or between MOF NPs and deposited PMMA. In comparison with the modified UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ NPs $(348~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , MOF-Cs $(314~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ showed a slight decrease in gravimetric BET surface areas due to the polymer deposition. Interestingly, the hierarchical MOF-Cs containing micropore $0.59\mathrm{nm}$ as well as mesopore $4.798\mathrm{nm}$ were further confirmed in pore-size distributions. The pore volume and mesopore volume of MOF-Cs were 0.142 and $0.036~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Hence, the relative proportion of meso- to micropores was $32.71\%$ in the MOF-Cs (Table S3, Supporting Information). However, PMMA-Cs had quite low surface area $(6.2\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and possessed the meso/macroporosity structure ( $50.36\ \mathrm{nm})$ . The pore volume and mesopore volume of PMMA-Cs are 0.024 and $0.024~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Therefore, the relative proportion of meso- to micropores was $100\%$ in the PMMA-Cs (Table S3, Supporting Information). And the PMMA-Cs owned nonuniform meso/macropore in pore-size distributions (Figure  S7, Supporting Information). The hierarchical pore structures owned by MOF-Cs were achieved through the self-assembly of MOF NPs. In which, the micro/mesoporosity from the assembled
MOF NPs can selectively tune the molecular transport, while the macroporosity of the interior hollow structure can be used to encapsulate diverse species. All these release the potential of MOF-Cs in controlled transport, effective encapsulation, compartmentalization effect, and communication skill.
## 2.3. The Controlled Transport Behavior of MOF-Cs
MOF-Cs can encapsulate diverse species and exhibit size-selective behavior. Here, rhodamine B (RB), water-soluble fluorescent molecules, was incorporated inside MOF-Cs during the self-assembly process to obtain RB $@$ MOF-Cs. In Figure 2A, the fluorescence image of ${\mathrm{RB}}@{\mathrm{MOF-Cs}}$ show strong fluorescence intensity $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=543\ \mathrm{nm})$ ). The reflection image is attributed to the reflected light of fluorescent molecules at the spherical surface. The reflection image of ${\mathrm{RB}}@{\mathrm{MOF-Cs}}$ is negligible, which indicates that RB is not on the surface of MOF-Cs and thus they are retained within MOF-Cs. The amount of RB inside MOF-Cs and PMMA-Cs were calculated to be about 6.2 and $4.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively (Figure  S8 and Table S4, Supporting Information). In contrast, MOF-Cs mixed with RB for 30 min revealed an opposite result. The reflection was more obvious implying that RB mostly absorb on the surface of MOF-Cs (Figure  2B). And the diffusion of RB $(15.9\times11.8\times9\mathrm{~\AA~})$ into MOF-Cs was prevented as illustrated by insignificant fluorescence. All these results suggest MOF-Cs are good candidates for effective encapsulation. Besides, quenching experiments using halogens with different molecular sizes as quenchers were conducted to investigate the size-selectivity of MOF-Cs.[25] The iodomethane molecules (Q1, $5.9\times4.5\times4.5$ Å) could get access to the pores of MOF-Cs (5.9 Å) and PMMA-Cs $(20.7\textup{\AA})$ for quenching RB, as confirmed by similar quenching efficiency of RB, $\mathrm{RB}@$ PMMA-Cs, and ${\mathrm{RB}}@{\mathrm{MOF-Cs}}$ (Figure  2C). By contrast, the ${\mathrm{RB}}@{\mathrm{MOF-Cs}}$ showed much lower quenching efficiency than RB or RB $@$ PMMA-Cs when treated with large and long-chain quencher (Q2: 5-(bromomethyl)undecane, $15.6\times13.5\times10~i$ Å) (Figure 2D). Presumably, the initial quenching is caused by few broken MOF-Cs. After that, the fluorescence intensity remains stable because RB cannot diffuse out and Q2 molecules cannot diffuse into MOF-Cs as a result of molecular sieving of MOF NPs. And in longer timescales, the fluorescence intensity of ${\mathrm{RB}}@{\mathrm{MOF-Cs}}$ remains stable owing to the unique structure of MOF-Cs (Figure S9, Supporting Information). These results also prove that RB mostly retains inside MOF-Cs and the resulted ${\mathrm{RB}}@{\mathrm{MOF-Cs}}$ exhibit size-selective behavior in quenchers with different molecular sizes. In comparison, RB $@$ PMMA-Cs displayed similar quenching efficiency as RB due to their relatively large pore. Moreover, fluorescence microscopy was also used to determine the release profiles of RB trapped within MOF-Cs and PMMA-Cs. It is observed that the release of RB $@$ MOF-Cs is negligible while RB $@$ PMMA-Cs manifest faster release rates (Figure S10, Supporting Information). Therefore, MOF-Cs represent enhanced barrier effect from MOF NPs.
## 2.4. Compartmentalization Effect inside MOF-Cs
Compartmentalization is an essential feature found in living cells to ensure biological processes occur without being affected by undesired external influences.[26] Inspired by this, MOF-Cs can be good candidates for a compartmentalized system which can protect encapsulated cargoes from external stimuli. Here, glucose oxidase (GOX), a model enzyme, was encapsulated inside MOF-Cs to look into the enzymatic property against protease (Pro) digestion (Figure S11, Supporting Information). According to Figure 3A, during the self-assembly process in oil-water surface, GOX was encapsulated inside MOF-Cs. Then, a certain amount of $\beta$ -glucose was added into the aqueous suspension of ${\mathrm{GOX}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}{\mathrm{-Cs}}$ . Therefore, ${\mathrm{GOX}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}{\mathrm{-Cs}}$ could induce the glucose oxidation reaction to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which was then used by horseradish peroxidase (HRP) to oxidize $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -azinobis(3-ethylbenzothiazoline-6-sulfonic acid) (ABTS) and generate a strong colorimetric signal $(\mathrm{ABTS^{+}})$ .[13] The glucose-oxidizing activities of GOX $@$ MOF-Cs were investigated by tracking the $\mathrm{\ABTS^{+}}$ with absorbance at $560\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Besides, GOX $@$ MOF-Cs protected the encapsulated GOX from protease (Pro), an enzyme which can induce the proteolysis of some enzymes and result in the decreased enzymatic property. The amount of GOX encapsulated inside MOF-Cs and PMMA-Cs were calculated about 5.3 and $5.1\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively (Figure S12 and Table S5, Supporting Information). The UV–vis data suggested that after the digestion by Pro, GOX $@$ MOF-Cs are highly resistant to digestion by Pro and retain more than $96\%$ of its initial activity (Figure  3B). However, GOX $@$ PMMA-Cs showed decreased enzyme activity (Figures S13 and S14, Supporting Information). Furthermore, GOX $@$ MOF-Cs exhibited high recyclability in the glucose oxidation (Figure 3C). These results indicated that MOF-Cs exhibited compartmentalization effect from the hierarchical pore structures. And, the high recyclability of MOF-Cs is originated from the special structure of MOF-Cs consisting of robust MOF as the shell and polymer as the supporting layer. The encapsulation strategy can also be extended to other kinds of enzymes such as HRP or the two enzymes together, which will entrust MOF-Cs with more flexibility and designability (Figures S11, S15, and S16, Supporting Information). In addition, this compartmentalization effect was also observed in the GOX HRP $@$ MOF-Cs obtained by the encapsulation of two-enzymes together inside
MOF-Cs (Figures S17 and S18, Supporting Information). Obviously, these enzymes $@$ MOF-Cs exhibited good recyclability in the enzymatic catalysis.
## 2.5. Communication between MOF-Cs
## 3. Conclusion
We have developed a simple but effective strategy to construct multifunctional MOF-Cs, which were fabricated by the selfassembly of presynthesized MOF NPs. Benefiting from this encapsulation strategy without rigid synthesis conditions of MOF such as high temperature, organic solvents and metal ions, MOF-Cs can encapsulate diverse active biomolecules and protect their activity (Table S6, Supporting Information). MOF-Cs owned the MOFs layer for controlling molecular transfer, the hollow structure for supplying confined reaction space and the ability of encapsulating different species for providing catalytic active sites. Notably, incompatible enzymes incorporated inside MOF-Cs, Pro $@$ MOF-Cs, and ${\mathrm{GOX}}@{\mathrm{MOF-Cs}}$ , also showed compartmentalization effect and could communicate between each other, resulting in tandem reaction. Hence, MOF-Cs may open up opportunities in the transportation, microreactor, and biotechnology.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials and Instrumentation: Commercial reagents were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (ACS grade) and used as received unless otherwise noted. MOF capsules were formed using a shear force created by a PowerGen 125 set T-701FBT10P Homogenizer. Powder XRD patterns were recorded with a Bruker AXS D8 Advance diffractometer using nickel-filtered Cu $K_{\alpha}$ radiation $(\lambda~=~7.5406~\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were taken by a JEOL JSM-7600 fieldemission SEM with an accelerating voltage of 5 kV. UV–vis spectra were recorded on a Shimadzu UV-2501 spectrophotometer. Fluorescence spectra were recorded on Hitachi F-4600 fluorescence Spectrometer. Nitrogen sorption studies were performed in a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 adsorption apparatus at 77 K up to 1 bar. The pore textural properties including specific Langmuir and BET surface area, pore volume, and pore size were obtained by analyzing nitrogen adsorption and desorption isotherms with Micromeritics ASAP 2020 built-in software. TGA were performed on a Q500 TGA (TA Instruments). All fluorescent images were acquired on Zeiss LSM880 NLO $2+1$ with BIG) Confocal Microscope System.
Synthesis of UiO $\partial_{-}G G_{-}N H_{2}$ NPs: Nanometer-sized $\mathsf{U i O-}66.\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ particles were synthesized according to Schaate’s method after minor revision.[22] In a typical synthesis, $_{0.08\mathrm{~g~}}$ zirconium chloride $(Z\mathsf{r C l}_{4}$ , $0.34~\mathsf{m m o l}_{.}^{\prime}$ ) and $0.42\textrm{g}$ benzoic acid $(3.43\ m m)^{\prime}$ ) were dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF in a $40~\mathrm{mL}$ glass sample vial by ultrasound for about 1 min, and then $0.06\ \&\ 2$ -aminoterephthalic acid $(2\cdot N\mathsf{H}_{2}\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ , $0.34~\mathsf{m m o l}$ ) was added into the solution above and dissolved by ultrasound for about $3\mathrm{\min}$ . The tightly capped vial was kept in an oven at $\mathsf{l}20^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ under static conditions. After cooling to room temperature, the precipitates were isolated through centrifugation. The as-synthesized particles were suspended in DMF $(6m L)$ . After $6\textup{h}$ of standing at room temperature, the suspension was centrifuged and the solvent was decanted off. This treatment was repeated three times. Afterward the same procedure was done with ethanol $(75m L)$ . The solids were dried at room temperature under vacuum for further surface hydrophobization.
Surface Hydrophobization of UiO-66- $N H_{2}$ NPs: In a preheated oil bath $(700~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , the $\mathsf{U i O-}66.\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ NPs was dispersed $(1~\mathsf{m L}/70~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{M O F})$ in heptanoic anhydride. After 10 min the sample was filtered immediately and washed three times with ethanol. Subsequently, samples were dried in vacuum.[23]
Preparation of MOF-Cs: The basic method used for the preparation of aqueous core microcapsules by internal phase separation was adapted here from Loxley and Vincent.[24] Briefly, $\overline{{15}}\:\mathsf{m g}$ PMMA was dissolved in an aqueous phase containing $448~\upmu\up L$ acetone and $32\upmu\up L$ water. The modified MOF NPs at the desired concentration (typically $0.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ were suspended in $4~\mathrm m\mathrm L$ dodecane, and then aqueous phase was added to the above-mentioned MOF suspension. After that, this system was sheared using a PowerGen 125 set Homogenizer Polytron homogenizer (T-701FBT10P) fitted with an $8~\mathsf{m m}$ dispersing tool at 22 000 rpm. At last, the MOF-Cs were obtained by removing the acetone using a rotary evaporator.
For corresponding PMMA-Cs, the MOF NPs were replaced by the $2\mathrm{\wt\%}$ Span 80. Briefly, $\overline{{15}}\:\mathsf{m g}$ PMMA was dissolved in an aqueous phase containing $448~\upmu\up L$ acetone and $32\upmu\up L$ water. $2\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{wt}\%$ Span 80 was suspended in $4m L$ dodecane, and then aqueous phase was added to the Span 80 suspension. After that, this system was sheared using a PowerGen 125 set Homogenizer Polytron homogenizer (T-701FBT10P) fitted with an $8\mathsf{m m}$ dispersing tool at 22 000 rpm. At last, the PMMA-Cs were obtained by removing the acetone using a rotary evaporator.
Procedures for the Encapsulation of Dyes inside MOF-Cs: In the preparation process of MOF-Cs, RB was dispersed in water and mixed with polymer in acetone. Then, the encapsulation of RB is achieved via the same procedure described in the preparation procedure of MOF-Cs. For comparison, RB can also be encapsulated inside PMMA-Cs.
Photoluminescence Quenching of rhodamine B inside MOF-Cs $\prime R B@$ MOF-Cs): The obtained RB@MOF-Cs (or RB@PMMA-Cs) were centrifuged and washed with ethanol. After that they were transferred into ethanol $(7m L)$ . Then, after the addition of the quenchers (Q1, Q2), the time-dependent concentrations of released dye in the continuous phase were evaluated using fluorescence spectrometer.
Photoluminescence Diffusing of RB@MOF-Cs: The obtained $\mathsf{R B}@$ MOF-Cs (or RB@PMMA-Cs) were centrifuged and washed with ethanol. After that they were transferred into ethanol (1 mL). The time-dependent concentrations of released dye in the continuous phase were evaluated using fluorescence spectrometer. In each case, a finite dye concentration was observed at the first time point because of the presence of the broken capsules in the dispersion that originated from the transfer process.
Procedures for the Encapsulation of Enzymes inside MOF-Cs: In the preparation process of MOF-Cs, enzymes (catalase, glucose oxidase, horseradish peroxidase, and protease) was dispersed in water and mixed with polymer in acetone. After that, the incorporation of enzymes is achieved by the same procedure described in of MOF-Cs. For the encapsulation of two kinds of enzymes, these enzymes were all dispersed in water like the above procedures. These enzymes can also be encapsulated into PMMA-Cs.
Catalytic Performances of Glucose Oxidase@MOF-Cs for Glucose Oxidation: The obtained glucose oxidase@MOF-Cs (G $;0\times@$ MOF-Cs) were centrifuged and washed with Tris buffer. After that they $(\approx5~\mathsf{m g})$ were transferred into $500~\upmu\up L$ Tris buffer. Then $50~\upmu\up L~6.6$ mg of $\beta$ -d-glucose solution in Tris buffer was added to the suspension. The sample was mixed with $500\upmu\up L$ of $1.8\times10^{-3}$ m ABTS solution and $0.0125~\mathsf{m g}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ horseradish peroxidase in a centrifuge tube and then incubated for $15\mathrm{\min}$ at room temperature and the reaction was monitored by UV–vis spectroscopy measuring the absorption at $470\ n m$ . Besides, after the digestion of protease (Pro) for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the GOX $\textcircled{0}$ MOF-Cs were added with $\beta\mathrm{.}$ -d-glucose for glucose oxidation. The GOX $@$ PMMA-Cs can also be prepared and experienced the same process with GOX@MOF-Cs.
Catalytic Performances of Horseradish Peroxidase@MOF-Cs for ABTS Reduction:Theobtainedhorseradishperoxidase $@$ MOF-Cs( ${\mathsf{H R P}}@{\mathsf{M O F}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{C s}})$ were centrifuged and washed with Tris buffer. After that they $(\approx5{\mathrm{~}}m g)$ were transferred into $500~\upmu\up L$ Tris buffer. The sample was well mixed with $500\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $1.8\times10^{-3}$ m ABTS solution and $0.5\times10^{-3}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ in a centrifuge tube and then incubated for $15\min$ at room temperature and the reaction was monitored by UV–vis spectroscopy measuring the absorption at $470\ \mathsf{n m}$ . Besides, after the digestion of Pro for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the HRP@PMOF-Cs then induced the ABTS reduction. The $H R P\ @P M M A-C s$ can also be prepared and experienced the same process with HRP $@$ MOF-Cs.
Catalytic Performances of $C O X H R P@M O F.C s$ or the Mixture of $C O X@$ MOF-Cs with HRP@MOF-Cs: Two-enzyme cascade reaction using GOX ${\mathsf{H R P}}\ @{\mathsf{M O F}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{C s}}$ or the mixture of GOX $@$ MOF-Cs with HRP@MOF-Cs were prepared as described above. The obtained GOX HRP $\textcircled{2}$ MOF-Cs or the mixture of ${\mathsf{C O X@M O F–C s}}$ with $\Hat{\mathsf{H R P}}(\overline{{\varrho}})$ MOF-Cs were centrifuged and washed with Tris buffer. After that they $(\approx5~\mathsf{m g})$ were transferred into $500~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ Tris buffer. In addition, $500~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of a $\bar{1.8}\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~m~}$ ABTS solution was added followed by the addition of $6.6~\mathsf{m g}$ of $\beta$ -d-glucose. The sample was well mixed in a centrifuge tube and then incubated for 15 min at room temperature and the reaction was monitored by UV– vis spectroscopy measuring the absorption at $470\ n m$ . Besides, after the digestion of Pro for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the GOX ${\mathsf{H R P}}@{\mathsf{M O F}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{C s}}$ or the mixture of GOX $@$ MOF-Cs with ${\mathsf{H R P}}@{\mathsf{M O F}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{C s}}$ were added with $\beta$ -d-glucose for glucose oxidation. The GOX HRP $@$ PMMA-Cs or the mixture of ${\mathsf{G O X}}({\overline{{a}}})$ PMMA-Cs with HRP $@$ PMMA-Cs can also be prepared and experienced the same process with above mentioned.
Catalytic Performances of Protease@MOF-Cs for Incompatible but Cascade Reaction: The obtained Protease $@$ MOF-Cs $({\mathsf{P r o@M O F-C s}})$ were centrifuged and washed with Tris buffer. After that they $(\approx5~\mathsf{m g})$ were transferred into $500\upmu\upiota$ Tris buffer. Then $50\upmu\updownarrow3.3\mathsf{m g}\uptau,2,3,4$ -tetra$\mathsf{O}$ -acetyl- $\cdot\beta$ -glucopyranose was added following the addition of $50~\upmu\up L$ ${\mathsf{G O X@M O F–C s}}$ which was also dispersed into $500~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ Tris buffer. The sample was well mixed with $500~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of $1.8\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}$ ABTS solution and HRP in a centrifuge tube and then incubated for 5 min at room temperature and the reaction was monitored by UV–vis spectroscopy measuring the absorption at $470\ n m$ . For comparison, the mixture of Pro@PMMA-Cs with GOX@PMMA-Cs can also be prepared and experienced the same process as above mentioned.
